 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Computational Cost:** The forward pass is approximately 10 times slower than the backward pass. The narrow-band method is applied in the backward computation whenever possible to save time [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Bias:** Using the narrow-band method for PSF optimization introduces bias because the threshold depends on current PSF values. Therefore, the exact FT must be used for PSF optimization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Explicit inversion of $(1 + \lambda L)^{-2}$ is memory-intensive for large vertex counts $n_v$. The linear system must be solved via sparse Cholesky decomposition [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Remeshing:** The method uses a multimaterial mesh-based surface tracking method to handle remeshing, collision detection, and topological transitions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Computational Cost:** The forward pass is approximately 10 times slower than the backward pass. The narrow-band method should be applied in the backward computation whenever possible to save time [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Bias:** Using the narrow-band method during PSF optimization introduces bias because the threshold depends on the current PSF values. Therefore, the exact FT must be used for PSF updates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Explicit inversion of the Laplacian term $(1 + \lambda L)^{-2}$ is memory-intensive for large vertex counts ($n_v$). Using sparse Cholesky decomposition is required to avoid this [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Initial Geometry:** The method requires an initial guess with the correct number of cells/topology (e.g., a foam-like mesh) [S1, unofficial].
 
